<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bujumbura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Usumbura</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bujumbura, called Usumbura until 1962, is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, on the north-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. Founded as a German military post in the 1890s, it was the administrative centre of Belgian Ruanda-Urundi and the capital of Burundi from independence until 2019, when the political capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bujumbura.jpg" alt="Bujumbura" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SteveRwanda · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BujumburaFromCathedral.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bujumbura stands on the north-eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, the second deepest lake in the world after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a>. The city lies at the mouth of the Ruzizi River and of the smaller Mutimbuzi, Ntahangwa, Muha and Kanyosha rivers. Its climate is tropical savanna, with a wet season from October to April. Because it is near the equator, temperatures vary little through the year, and the moderate altitude keeps them lower than might be expected, with highs around 29 degrees and lows around 19.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bujumbura grew from a small village after it became a military post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was made the administrative centre and de facto capital of the Belgian League of Nations mandate of Ruanda-Urundi, and its name was changed from Usumbura to Bujumbura on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>'s independence in 1962. The Belgian colonial period left a mark on the city centre, whose buildings of the first half of the 20th century include elements of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">Modernism</a> and Art Deco, many still visible in the historical centre.</p><p>Since independence Bujumbura has been the scene of repeated fighting between the country's two main ethnic groups. During the Burundian Civil War of the 1990s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> militias opposed the Tutsi-dominated Burundi Army, and the battles destroyed hospitals, administrative buildings, the University of Burundi and other schools in the city, severely crippling the local economy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bujumbura is the largest city and main port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>, its economic capital and, until 2019, its political capital. Parliament voted on 16 January 2019 to return the political capital's status to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, leaving Bujumbura as the centre of commerce.</p><p>The Port of Bujumbura is the largest on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. Burundi depends on its neighbours for access to the ocean, and about 80 per cent of its external trade travels by the lake and then by road or rail through Tanzania, Congo or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>. The port ships most of the country's chief export, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> ore. Nearby sights include Rusizi National Park and the Livingstone-Stanley Monument at Mugere, which marks where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> visited 14 days after their first meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujiji">Ujiji</a> in Tanzania.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bujumbura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Germany set up a military post at <b>Usumbura</b> on the lake shore in 1896, Mwezi refused to submit for seven years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Mutara III died suddenly at <b>Usumbura</b> in July 1959, his half-brother Kigeli V Ndahindurwa succeeded amid rising tension, and violence in November 1959 began the Hutu revolution, which Belgian authorities supported.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-rwanda">Kingdom of Rwanda · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its administrative centre was <b>Usumbura</b>, now Bujumbura.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruanda-urundi">Ruanda-Urundi</a></li></ul></section>
